sample (Figure 1b) still exhibits the characteristic $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peaks along with the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ peaks, which indicates incomplete conversion of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . The intensity of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ diffraction peak is substantially reduced with longer dipping time up to $20~\mathrm{min}$ . The $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak disappears completely with $30\ \mathrm{\min}$ dipping. The typical scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of planar $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ ( $20~\mathrm{min}$ dipping/ conversion) films are compared in Figure 1, panels $\mathsf{\Lambda}_{\mathsf{c}}$ and ${\textsc{d}},$ respectively. The morphology of this $20~\mathrm{min}$ -dipping $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film is similar to that of a film with shorter dipping time $\cdot6\mathrm{min};$ Figure S1, Supporting Information). The planar $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film appears porous with a coarse top surface, which may be caused by the shrinkage of the precursor film during the drying process.11,15,22 The resulting $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film is also full of tiny pinholes, and its surface is covered with rough perovskite nanocrystals; this appearance is similar to that of the perovskite capping layer grown on a mesoporous substrate using two-step sequential deposition.8,12,13,23 The increased roughness of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film compared to the initial $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film is attributable to the space expansion during the MAI intercalation/reaction step. Taken together, the XRD and absorption results (Figure 1a,b) illustrate the difficulty in preparing planar $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ thin films from $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ using the two-step sequential solution deposition approach. This challenge has been discussed in detail elsewhere.24 The effect of varying MAI dipping time on the device characteristics is shown in Figure S2 with the corresponding photovoltaic parameters listed in Table S1. The cell efficiency increases significantly with longer dipping time, from $3.15\%$ at $0.5\ \mathrm{min}$ to $6.11\%$ at $2~\mathrm{min}$ to $9.30\%$ at $6~\mathrm{min},$ , which is consistent with the more complete conversion of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . However, when the dipping time is further increased to $20\ \mathrm{\min},$ the device efficiency drops to only $1.29\%$ even though the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film with $20~\mathrm{min}$ dipping has much reduced $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ with stronger light absorption than the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film with $6~\mathrm{min}$ dipping. Thus, it is evident that simply lengthening the reaction time to complete the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ -to- $.\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ conversion presents a challenge for the typical two-step sequential solution processing. The long dipping/reaction duration could cause damage to the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ layer resulting from the possible back extraction of MAI from $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}},^{21}$ and thus it deteriorates the device performance. Therefore, it is critical to develop a strategy to speed up the conversion process (or minimize the MAI solution dipping time) for preparing planar $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films.   
To address the challenges associated with the incomplete $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$
conversion of and rough perovskite surface discussed above, we examined the effect of using a new precursor (mixture of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and ${\boldsymbol{x}}\mathbf{MAI}$ with $x$ varying from 0.1−0.3) during the first deposition step. The resulting precursor film is denoted as $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{MAI}$ . Even in the presence of MAI, the deposited $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{MAI}$ films still look yellowish, without any indication of the formation of perovskite $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . The UV−vis absorption spectra (Figure 2a) of the ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ precursor films exhibit an absorption onset near ${510}~\mathrm{nm},$ , which is characteristic for $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . The sharp absorption edge associated with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ becomes less clear with increasing MAI. When $x=$ 0.3, the baseline of the absorption spectrum increases substantially, indicating an enhanced light-scattering effect. Figure 2, panel b compares the XRD patterns of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{\bar{M}A I}$ (x: $0{-}0.3)$ precursor films. In the absence of MAI, the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film displays a strong characteristic XRD peak at about $12.5^{\circ}$ . The addition of $x\mathbf{MAI}$ into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ during the first deposition step does not result in any detectable $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ diffraction peaks such as the main (110) peak near $14^{\circ}$ . This is consistent with the appearance of the yellow color and the corresponding absorption spectra. The intensity of the main $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ diffraction peak $(\sim12.5^{\circ})$ decreases substantially with increasing amount of MAI in the precursor. This observation suggests that a partial incorporation of MAI in the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film decreases the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ crystallinity in the ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ films (compared to the pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film) without forming the perovskite $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ phase. It is noteworthy that there are some unknown peaks formed below $10^{\circ}$ when MAI is used in the precursor film. By comparing the XRD patterns of the ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ precursor films to those of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ and MAI (Figure S8), we can attribute these peaks below $10^{\circ}$ to the formation of certain complexes, which become stronger for $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}0.3\mathrm{MAI}$ . Similar observation of complex formation was discussed previously.11
Figure 3 panels $\mathsf{a{-}d}$ show the typical SEM images of top views of the ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ $\mathbf{\chi}_{x}^{\prime}=0.1$ , 0.15, 0.2, and 0.3) precursor films. These films have very different morphologies than the pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film, and their morphologies vary with the amount of MAI used in the precursor. When $x\leq0.2,$ , introducing MAI into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ leads to the formation of a smoother ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ film with fewer pinholes than the pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film. However, the morphology of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}0.3\mathrm{MAI}$ film becomes even coarser than that of pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film, which is consistent with the higher absorption baseline of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}0.3\mathrm{MAI}$ film (Figure 2a). Figure
3, panels $^\mathrm{e{-h}}$ show the typical SEM images of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films prepared from ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ film after the second dipping/ conversion step. It is worth noting that the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films fabricated from ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ $\stackrel{\prime}{x}=0.1{-}0.2\stackrel{\cdot}{_{\iota}}$ ) are nearly pinholefree and show a relatively uniform, smooth surface, especially for the 0.15MAI sample, which is significantly different from the relatively rough $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films prepared from the pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film. The grain sizes of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films also show a dependence on the amount of MAI used in the precursor. When $x=0.1$ , the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film mainly consists of relatively small $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ nanocrystals with sizes less than $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . When $0.15\mathrm{-}0.2\$ MAI is used, the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films are composed of many relatively large ${\sim}500~\mathrm{nm})$ crystals filled with ${\sim}200~\mathrm{nm}$ small crystals; such film morphology is comparable to that of the vapor-phase-grown perovskite films. This result is also consistent with the absence of an absorption baseline increase during the sequential deposition, as shown in Figure 2, panel d. When started with the relatively coarse $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}0.3\mathrm{MAI}$ film, the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film looks similar to that prepared from the pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film. These results clearly demonstrate that our new $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · xMAI-based two-step sequential deposition allows (to a certain degree) the control of the morphology of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film.
In the typical two-step sequential solution deposition of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3},$ the layer-structured $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ crystal is intercalated by MAI to form the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3},$ as shown in Figure 4, panel a. As discussed above, the significant phase transformation normally leads to about a factor of two volume expansion per formula during the second conversion step .12,21 To help understand the effect of adding MAI (during the first step of deposition) on the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film formation, the thickness (or volume) expansion ratios of converting $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ·xMAI to $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films were examined by using a surface profiler. Figure 4, panel b shows the result of the expansion ratios for the films prepared with different amounts $(x)$ of MAI from $0{-}0.3$ . The ratio continuously decreases with increasing amount of MAI, which could be accounted for by the following hypothesis. For the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{MAI}$ films, the added MAI is likely incorporated into the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ matrix and has partially pre-expanded the volume of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ matrix. The degree of this pre-expansion depends on the amount of MAI used during the first step of deposition. This preexpansion decreases the final film expansion ratio with increasing MAI amount. Assuming the pre-expansion ratio per formula of reactant MAI is the same as the expansion ratio in the standard conversion process with pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2},$ the expansion ratio of the final $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film thickness $\left(d_{\mathrm{f}}\right)$ to the thickness of the initial ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ film $(d_{\mathrm{i}})$ can be calculated with the expression $d_{\mathrm{f}}/d_{\mathrm{i}}=m/[1+(m-1)x],$ , where $m$ is the expansion ratio. Best fits of the data to this express yield $m=$ $2.02\pm0.07$ ; the best-fitted line is shown in Figure 4, panel b. The derivation of the expression for the expansion ratio is given in the Supporting Information (eqs S1−S3). It is interesting that the expansion is also about two, which suggests that the pre-expansion to form ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ and the standard expansion to form $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ have essentially the same volume expansion ratio. It is also interesting to note that the XRD intensity of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak is much reduced even when the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ phase still constitutes the majority of the ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ precursor film (e.g., $x$ $=0.15$ ; Figure 2b). Thus, the addition of a small amount of MAI results in several features that favor the rapid conversion reaction during the second step. The features of using $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ · ${\boldsymbol{x}}\mathbf{MAI}$ matrix include the reduced $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ crystallinity, the preexpansion of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{MAI},$ and the controllable morphology by varying the amount of MAI in ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}.$ Moreover, using different amounts of MAI allows the control of the pre-expansion of the ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ precursor film, which gives rise to a distinctive morphology, as shown in Figure 3, and also affects the final photovoltaic performance of perovskite solar cells based on the planar $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films, as discussed next.
Figure 5 shows the typical photocurrent density−voltage $\left(J-\right.$ V) curves of planar $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ solar cells prepared using different ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ $\left(x\colon0{-}0.3\right)$ under simulated one-sun illumination. The details of photovoltaic parameters of all these devices including the statistical analysis (mean values and standard deviations) for each type of device is given in Table S2 in the Support Information. These $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathbf{M}\mathrm{\bar{A}I}$ films were dipped in the MAI solution for $2~\mathrm{min}$ to form the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ layers, which were adjusted to about $250~\mathrm{nm}$ thick. In the absence of MAI (i.e., using the standard $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ precursor), the device shows a short-circuit photocurrent density $(J_{s c})$ of $8.85~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , opencircuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of $0.969\mathrm{~V~},$ fill factor (FF) of 0.712, and overall conversion efficiency $(\eta)$ of $6.11\%$ . The cell efficiency increases significantly when $0.1\mathrm{-}0.2\$ MAI along with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is used during the first-step deposition, especially when 0.15 MAI is used. A typical efficiency for the 0.15 MAI-based device is increased to $15.62\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $19.89\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 1.065, and FF of 0.738. The performance improvement is largely determined by the increased $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value associated with the enhanced $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ conversion process and less perovskite damage resulting from the minimized interaction with the IPA solvent. The $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF values for the devices based on $0.1\mathrm{-}0.2\$ MAI are also significantly larger than those of the cells based on pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (Table S2). However, when $0.3\mathrm{\}\mathrm{MAI}$ is used, all device parameters are reduced, with a typical $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $13.87~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $0.967~\mathrm{V}_{;}$ , and FF of 0.668, yielding an efficiency of $8.98\%$ . This is presumably due to the coarser morphology of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ when $0.3\mathrm{MAI}$ is used because there is no $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residue found in the perovskite film. The significant drop in cell performance of the 0.3 MAI-based device is consistent with its much reduced recombination resistance (or faster recombination) compared to the $0.1\mathrm{-}0.2\$ MAI-based cells (Figure S4). Using 0.15 MAI, the best cell efficiency obtained is $17.22\%$ (Figure S5a) with its $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value consistent with the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectrum (inset of Figure S5a). The maximum power output of this device stabilizes at an efficiency of about $16.9\%$ (Figure S5b), which is in good agreement with the value obtained from the $J{-}V$ measurement.
In summary, we report the use of a new composition precursor $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathbf{M}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{I}$ consisting of mixed $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and partial MAI (molar ratio $1{:}x,$ where $x$ varies from 0.1−0.3) to replace the pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ used in the two-step sequential solution deposition of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . In comparison to the standard two-step approach using pure $\mathrm{PbI}_{2},$ , the use of additive MAI during the first step of deposition leads to about 10-fold faster $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ formation without any $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residue during the (second) MAI intercalation step and much improved device performance when $0.1\mathrm{-}0.2\$ MAI is used. The morphology of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film depends on the relative amount of MAI used in the ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ precursor films (during the first-step deposition), and it is generally smoother when $0.1{-}0.2\ \mathrm{MAI}$ is used. The addition of a small amount of MAI is found to lead to the reduced crystallinity of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and the pre-expansion of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ into ${\mathrm{PbI}}_{2}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{MAI}}$ with adjustable morphology, which favor the complete conversion reaction within a short period of time and thus minimize the negative impact of the IPA solvent on the integrity of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film during the (second) dipping/conversion step. Our results suggest that this novel first-step precursor $\left(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{MAI}\right)$ is promising for facile preparation of high-quality planar $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film with controllable morphologies via two-step sequential solution deposition to fabricate high-performance perovskite solar cells.
